# What a round clang refused asks the header about the names this program
# declared, in one more clang run, and how the answer is read (design.md
# §4.19; CLAUDE.md §7's named exception; defect 152, 2026-10-02).
#
# WHY ONE MORE RUN, AND ONLY AFTER A REFUSAL. The first round's words are the
# verdict and they stay first: the probe judges a fortified function and a name
# that is both a function and a macro as it always has (panel 092). But some of
# what clang says cannot be put on the author's line from those words alone. An
# `extern` with no parameter has no probe, and its result assertion carries no
# `#line`, since every byte of the emitted C stays as it was, so `function
# errno() -> i32` against `errno.h` drew *called object type 'int' is not a
# function or function pointer* at a line of the generated unit and at the
# call, and `build` said `internal error`, exit 2: the compiler blaming itself
# for the author's declaration. This unit says each such call again at the
# declaration's own line, where `emit/ffi.hero`'s readers look already, and
# asks what decides the message: whether a name the header has is a value. It
# is compiled only by a build that has already failed, so a program that builds
# never pays for it (`cli/header_ask.hero` runs it).
#
# WHAT IS SAID AGAIN, per `extern` function of the program's own files:
#
# - with no parameter, the call, `(void)errno();`: the same call the result
#   assertion and every call site make, so it compiles wherever they do, and
#   clang's word about it (an object, a value, a type, a count) lands on the
#   declaration;
# - with parameters, the name alone, `(void)my_int;`: the probe already makes
#   the call at the declaration's line, and one shape gets past it, a TYPE
#   name, which `(my_int)(a0)` reads as a cast.
#
# Both compile for every correct binding, measured on a header of the lane's
# own and on `errno.h`, `stdio.h`, `string.h` and `unistd.h`, so a refusal the
# first round already explained is told exactly as before.
#
# THE QUESTIONS ANSWER IN THE FILE NAME. Each is written under `#line 1
# "hero-ask-<kind> <name>"` (the ffi-pragmatist's spelling at panel 185), so
# clang's line about it carries the question and never reaches a reader that
# gates on the program's own files. An answer is POSITIVE evidence: a value is
# `static assertion failed` under its question, never a silence, so a clang
# that could not run answers nothing rather than yes. A value is what
# `__builtin_constant_p` holds while compiling, which is the very question a
# `constant` member asks (`emit/extern_assert.hero`): an enumerator and
# `#define BUFSIZ 1024` are values, `errno`, `stdin` and a static variable are
# not, and neither is a function's name, measured.
#
# **And whether a name is a MACRO** (panel 185 R1, ratified 2026-10-02):
# `#ifdef` under its question, and a `#warning` is the yes. A macro has no
# parameter types for clang to hold a declaration to, so the probe's
# parenthesised call refuses a macro-only name as *undeclared* (panel 092), and
# until that day the author was told the header has no such name, which is
# false of `sys/wait.h` and `WEXITSTATUS`. Every name of a group is asked, a
# `constant`'s too, because it costs one line in a run already paid for; the
# answer is read only where clang could not reach the name as anything else,
# so a fortified function and a name that is both a function and a macro keep
# the first round's verdict.
#
# **And whether a record's tag is a TYPE NAME** (panel 185 R2): `typedef T
# hero_q;`, the ffi-pragmatist's question, tells a type name, a real tag and
# neither apart, where `sizeof` called a function's name a typedef and could
# not tell a tag from a misspelling (the critic's first pass). clang recovers
# from both refusals, so an assertion after the typedef fails every time it is
# reached: it is the positive evidence that the question was asked, and the
# typedef's own words are the answer, none for a type name, *must use 'struct'
# tag* for a tag, *unknown type name* for neither, and since lane land186
# (2026-10-02, defect 157 widened) *must use 'union' tag* and *'enum' tag* for
# a tag of those kinds, which until then answered `type`, a typedef, falsely. Defect 145 left the note two
# readings where clang showed no spelling of the type; this makes it one.

use ast
use source
use ir/containers
use emit/externs as emit_externs
use emit/writer
use emit/ffi_lookup
use parse
use resolve
use checker
use ir/lower

# The file name every question is written under, and the one for a value.
constant ASK: str
    "hero-ask-"

constant VALUE: str
    "hero-ask-value "

constant MACRO: str
    "hero-ask-macro "

constant TYPE: str
    "hero-ask-type "

# What the run answered: clang's lines about what was said again, each at a
# declaration's line, for the readers to read beside the round's own; the
# names the header holds as a value; the names it defines as a macro; and what
# each record's tag is, `type`, `tag` or `neither`.
record Asked
    said: str
    values: {str: bool}
    macros: {str: bool}
    types: {str: str}

# The answer of a run that was not made: nothing said, nothing a value or a
# macro, no tag answered.
function nothing() -> Asked
    none: {str: bool} = {}
    unasked: {str: str} = {}
    return Asked(said: "", values: none, macros: none, types: unasked)

# The unit's C: the program's headers, then what is said again and asked of
# each `extern` function its own files declare. A library extern is not asked
# about: no line of the library is a line the author can be told about
# (`emit/ffi_site.hero`'s `location` refuses one).
function unit(p: containers.Program, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl]) -> str
    out: [str] @ []

    for header in emit_externs.headers(p, s, decls)
        out @ out.push("#include <" + header + ">")

    index: i64 @ 0

    for f in p.functions
        if emit_externs.extern_spans(decls, f).header.is_err()
            continue
        start = decls[f.decl].name.start

        if source.is_library(s, start)
            continue
        name = source.span_text(s, decls[f.decl].name)
        out @ out.push(directive(file: MACRO + name, line: 1))
        out @ out.push("#ifdef " + name)
        out @ out.push("#warning hero-ask")
        out @ out.push("#endif")

        # A constant asks the rest of its questions in the first round.
        if emit_externs.is_extern_constant(decls, f)
            continue
        at = source.locate(s, start)
        said: str @ name

        if f.params.len() == 0
            said @ name + "()"
        out @ out.push(directive(file: at.file, line: at.line))
        out @ out.push(
            "__attribute__((unused)) static void hero_ask_" + index.to_str() + "(void) { (void)" + said + "; }"
        )
        out @ out.push(directive(file: VALUE + name, line: 1))
        out @ out.push("_Static_assert(!__builtin_constant_p(" + name + "), \"hero-ask\");")
        index @ index + 1

    # Whether each group record's field names a header macro (lane land186,
    # 2026-10-02): a field the header has under a macro is a member C reaches
    # (libc's `sa_handler`), and a misspelt one that names some other macro
    # is no member at all, which clang can only say as a syntax error.
    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .record_decl rec =>
                if !rec.header.is_err() && !source.is_library(s, decl.name.start)
                    for field in rec.fields
                        name = source.span_text(s, field.name)
                        out @ out.push(directive(file: MACRO + name, line: 1))
                        out @ out.push("#ifdef " + name)
                        out @ out.push("#warning hero-ask")
                        out @ out.push("#endif")
            .constant_decl | .function_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

    # Each group record's tag, but `void`, which only a handle carries.
    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .record_decl rec =>
                if !rec.header.is_err() && !rec.tag.is_err() && !source.is_library(s, decl.name.start)
                    tag = source.span_text(s, rec.tag.must())

                    if tag != "void"
                        out @ out.push(directive(file: TYPE + tag, line: 1))
                        out @ out.push("typedef " + tag + " hero_q_" + index.to_str() + ";")
                        out @ out.push("_Static_assert(sizeof(hero_q_" + index.to_str() + " *) == 0, \"hero-ask\");")
                        index @ index + 1
            .constant_decl | .function_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

    return out.join("\n") + "\n"

# A `#line` spelled as the emitter spells it (`emit/writer.hero`), so a line
# said again names the file exactly as the probe's does.
function directive(file: str, line: i64) -> str
    return "#line " + line.to_str() + " \"" + writer.escaped_path(file) + "\""

# clang's answer, read: a question's line is an answer, the rest was said again.
# ORDER: none, the walk over `reached` fills a map, and nothing reads its order.
function read(stderr: str) -> Asked
    said: [str] @ []
    values: {str: bool} @ {}
    macros: {str: bool} @ {}
    reached: {str: bool} @ {}
    refused: {str: str} @ {}

    for row in ffi_lookup.lines(text: stderr)
        if !begins(text: row, prefix: ASK)
            said @ said.push(row)
            continue

        if begins(text: row, prefix: VALUE) && ffi_lookup.has(text: row, needle: ffi_lookup.FAILED)
            values[asked_name(row: row, prefix: VALUE)] @ true

        if begins(text: row, prefix: MACRO) && ffi_lookup.has(text: row, needle: "warning: hero-ask")
            macros[asked_name(row: row, prefix: MACRO)] @ true

        if begins(text: row, prefix: TYPE)
            name = asked_name(row: row, prefix: TYPE)

            if ffi_lookup.has(text: row, needle: ffi_lookup.FAILED)
                reached[name] @ true
            else if ffi_lookup.has(text: row, needle: "must use 'struct' tag")
                refused[name] @ "tag"
            else if ffi_lookup.has(text: row, needle: "must use 'union' tag")
                refused[name] @ "union"
            else if ffi_lookup.has(text: row, needle: "must use 'enum' tag")
                refused[name] @ "enum"
            else if ffi_lookup.has(text: row, needle: "unknown type name")
                refused[name] @ "neither"

    types: {str: str} @ {}

    for name in keys(reached)
        types[name] @ refused[name].default("type")

    return Asked(said: said.join("\n"), values: values, macros: macros, types: types)

# The name a question's line asks about: what its file name holds after the
# question's own words, up to the colon that opens the position.
function asked_name(row: str, prefix: str) -> str
    cs = row.chars()
    index: i64 @ prefix.chars().len()
    kept: [str] @ []

    while index < cs.len() && cs[index] != ":"
        kept @ kept.push(cs[index])
        index @ index + 1

    return kept.join("")

function begins(text: str, prefix: str) -> bool
    at = ffi_lookup.char_index_of(text: text, needle: prefix)
    return !at.is_err() && at.must() == 0

## Tests

test "the unit says each extern's call again at its line, and asks whether it is a value"
    text = "extern \"errno.h\"\n    function errno() -> i32\n    function strerror(code: i32) -> cstr\n    constant EDOM: i64\n\nfunction main()\n    print(EDOM)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
    c = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 0
    low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
    got = unit(low.program, s, parsed.decls)
    assert ffi_lookup.has(text: got, needle: "#include <errno.h>\n")

    # No parameter: the call itself, at the declaration's line.
    assert ffi_lookup.has(
        text: got,
        needle: "#line 2 \"t.hero\"\n__attribute__((unused)) static void hero_ask_0(void) { (void)errno(); }\n"
    )

    # Parameters: the name alone, since the probe makes the call.
    assert ffi_lookup.has(
        text: got,
        needle: "#line 3 \"t.hero\"\n__attribute__((unused)) static void hero_ask_1(void) { (void)strerror; }\n"
    )
    assert ffi_lookup.has(
        text: got,
        needle: "#line 1 \"hero-ask-value errno\"\n_Static_assert(!__builtin_constant_p(errno), \"hero-ask\");\n"
    )

    # Whether each is a macro, a constant's name too; and a constant asks the
    # rest of its questions in the first round.
    assert ffi_lookup.has(
        text: got,
        needle: "#line 1 \"hero-ask-macro errno\"\n#ifdef errno\n#warning hero-ask\n#endif\n"
    )
    assert ffi_lookup.has(text: got, needle: "#line 1 \"hero-ask-macro EDOM\"\n#ifdef EDOM\n")
    assert !ffi_lookup.has(text: got, needle: "(void)EDOM")
    assert !ffi_lookup.has(text: got, needle: "hero-ask-value EDOM")

test "a record's tag is asked whether it is a type name, and `void` is not asked"
    text = "extern \"own.h\"\n    record Q tag only_tagg\n        a: i32\n    record H tag void\n\nfunction touch(q: Q, h: H)\n    _ = q\n    _ = h\n\nfunction main()\n    print(1)\n"
    parsed = parse.parse(text)
    s = source.new_source(name: "t.hero", text: text)
    out = resolve.resolve(parsed.tree, s, parsed.decls, parsed.uses)
    c = checker.check(parsed.tree, out, s, parsed.decls)
    assert c.diagnostics.len() == 0
    low = lower.lower(parsed.tree, out, c, s, parsed.decls)
    got = unit(low.program, s, parsed.decls)
    assert ffi_lookup.has(
        text: got,
        needle: "#line 1 \"hero-ask-type only_tagg\"\ntypedef only_tagg hero_q_0;\n_Static_assert(sizeof(hero_q_0 *) == 0, \"hero-ask\");\n"
    )
    assert !ffi_lookup.has(text: got, needle: "typedef void")

test "an answer is read from the question's file name, and only a failed assertion is a value"
    stderr = "t.hero:2:67: error: called object type 'int' is not a function or function pointer\nhero-ask-value BUFSIZ:1:16: error: static assertion failed due to requirement '!__builtin_constant_p(1024)': hero-ask\nhero-ask-value nosuch:1:38: error: use of undeclared identifier 'nosuch'\nhero-ask-macro WEXITSTATUS:2:2: warning: hero-ask [-W#warnings]\n2 errors generated."
    got = read(stderr)
    assert !got.values["BUFSIZ"].is_err()
    assert !got.macros["WEXITSTATUS"].is_err()
    assert got.macros.len() == 1

    # A tag's three answers, each only where the question was reached.
    tags = "hero-ask-type anon_s:2:16: error: static assertion failed due to requirement 'sizeof(anon_s *) == 0': hero-ask\nhero-ask-type only_tag:1:9: error: must use 'struct' tag to refer to type 'only_tag'\nhero-ask-type only_tag:2:16: error: static assertion failed due to requirement 'sizeof(struct only_tag *) == 0': hero-ask\nhero-ask-type only_tagg:1:9: error: unknown type name 'only_tagg'\nhero-ask-type only_tagg:2:16: error: static assertion failed due to requirement 'sizeof(int *) == 0': hero-ask\nhero-ask-type cut:1:9: error: unknown type name 'cut'\n"
    typed = read(tags).types
    assert typed["anon_s"].must() == "type"
    assert typed["only_tag"].must() == "tag"
    assert typed["only_tagg"].must() == "neither"
    assert typed["cut"].is_err()

    # A question clang could not ask is no answer.
    assert got.values["nosuch"].is_err()
    assert got.values.len() == 1

    # What was said again stays, for the readers; no answer's line does.
    assert ffi_lookup.has(text: got.said, needle: "t.hero:2:67: error: called object type 'int'")
    assert !ffi_lookup.has(text: got.said, needle: "hero-ask")
    assert nothing().said == ""
